{
 "thread": "iBjP4Qq7aP4",
 "subject": "rstan and packages",
 "messages_listed": 9,
 "last_activity": "2015-07-29T15:43:30Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "iQyW9uO68QIJ",
   "from": "Sebastian Weber",
   "author": "97d82fedb7016adb",
   "date": "2015-07-24T06:48:29Z",
   "subject": "rstan and packages",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi!<div><br></div><div>I am planning to include stan models in R package and have a couple of questions:</div><div><br></div><div>- How can I make sure that the binary versions of the package come along with a pre-compiled stan binary?</div><div>- Is there a recommended way to do this?</div><div>- Should I use the command-line interface or the rstan interface?</div><div>- And which options make 100% sure that stan does not output anything (the open_progress=FALSE does not seem to be sufficient)</div><div><br></div><div>Many thanks in advance!</div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div>Sebastian</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "RQ9_fFQYuxUJ",
   "from": "Ben Goodrich",
   "author": "743bbacc59f4b25f",
   "date": "2015-07-24T13:35:54Z",
   "subject": "Re: rstan and packages",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Friday, July 24, 2015 at 2:48:29 AM UTC-4, Sebastian Weber wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I am planning to include stan models in R package and have a couple of questions:<div><br></div><div>- How can I make sure that the binary versions of the package come along with a pre-compiled stan binary?</div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>You have to compile them<br> </div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>- Is there a recommended way to do this?</div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>Download the .zip file but put it under inst/include of a copy of an unzipped rstan_2.7.0-1.tar.gz (not under the include/ directory of your installed rstan)<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">setwd</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;/path/to/inst/include/example-models&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>models </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"font-family:monospace\"><span style=\"color:#000000;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"color:#000\">dir</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">getwd</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> pattern </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;stan$&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> full</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">names</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> recursive </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>library</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">paralell</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></span><span style=\"color:#000\">options</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mc</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">cores </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">8</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#800\"># or however many cores you have available</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>output </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mclapply</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">models</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mc</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">silent </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>                   FUN </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">function</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">m</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">try</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rstan</span><span style=\"color:#660\">::</span><span style=\"color:#000\">stan_model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">m</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> auto_write </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>Then rebuild the rstan package via R CMD build<br><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>- Should I use the command-line interface or the rstan interface?</div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>rstan<br> </div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>- And which options make 100% sure that stan does not output anything (the open_progress=FALSE does not seem to be sufficient)</div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>The open_progress to sampling() or stan() is only relevant if you are running the model. Ostensibly the mc.silent argument suppresses stdout, but it is not as if the &quot;COMPILING MODEL X NOW&quot; statements hurt anything. You could also background the R process doing this.<br><br>Ben<br><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "kbxGtXa--VkJ",
   "from": "Sebastian Weber",
   "author": "97d82fedb7016adb",
   "date": "2015-07-24T13:52:53Z",
   "subject": "Re: rstan and packages",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Ben!<div><br></div><div>Well, your answer is great - as always; and I will likely use this. Very sweet!</div><div><br></div><div>However, I did have in mind to deploy my own library, which uses some stan models behind the scenes to do its work. So from what I get from you, is that I could for example setup a script which compiles my model like</div><div><br></div><div>stan_model &lt;- stan(&quot;my_internal_library_model.stan&quot;, chains=0)</div><div><br></div><div>and then do</div><div><br></div><div>writeRDS(stan_model, file=&quot;inst/data/my_model.rda&quot;)</div><div><br></div><div>Then in my R library Ioad the rda file the usual way and have immediately a ready to run Stan model, correct? Thanks.</div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div>Sebastian</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br><br>On Friday, July 24, 2015 at 3:35:54 PM UTC+2, Ben Goodrich wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">On Friday, July 24, 2015 at 2:48:29 AM UTC-4, Sebastian Weber wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I am planning to include stan models in R package and have a couple of questions:<div><br></div><div>- How can I make sure that the binary versions of the package come along with a pre-compiled stan binary?</div></div></blockquote><div><br>You have to compile them<br> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>- Is there a recommended way to do this?</div></div></blockquote><div><br>Download the .zip file but put it under inst/include of a copy of an unzipped rstan_2.7.0-1.tar.gz (not under the include/ directory of your installed rstan)<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">setwd</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;/path/to/inst/include/example-models&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>models </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"font-family:monospace\"><span style=\"color:#000000;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"color:#000\">dir</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">getwd</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> pattern </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;stan$&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> full</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">names</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> recursive </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>library</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">paralell</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></span><span style=\"color:#000\">options</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mc</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">cores </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">8</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#800\"># or however many cores you have available</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>output </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mclapply</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">models</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mc</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">silent </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>                   FUN </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">function</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">m</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">try</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rstan</span><span style=\"color:#660\">::</span><span style=\"color:#000\">stan_model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">m</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> auto_write </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>Then rebuild the rstan package via R CMD build<br><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>- Should I use the command-line interface or the rstan interface?</div></div></blockquote><div><br>rstan<br> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>- And which options make 100% sure that stan does not output anything (the open_progress=FALSE does not seem to be sufficient)</div></div></blockquote><div><br>The open_progress to sampling() or stan() is only relevant if you are running the model. Ostensibly the mc.silent argument suppresses stdout, but it is not as if the &quot;COMPILING MODEL X NOW&quot; statements hurt anything. You could also background the R process doing this.<br><br>Ben<br><br></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "Qq52HTPVzrUJ",
   "from": "Ben Goodrich",
   "author": "743bbacc59f4b25f",
   "date": "2015-07-24T13:59:00Z",
   "subject": "Re: rstan and packages",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Friday, July 24, 2015 at 9:52:53 AM UTC-4, Sebastian Weber wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">However, I did have in mind to deploy my own library, which uses some stan models behind the scenes to do its work. So from what I get from you, is that I could for example setup a script which compiles my model like<div><br></div><div>stan_model &lt;- stan(&quot;my_internal_library_model.stan&quot;, chains=0)</div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>The rstan 2.7.0 package looks for a serialized stanmodel object next to the .stan file, not a stanfit object. So, you want to call stan_model() on a .stan file with auto_write = TRUE, and that does a saveRDS().<br><br>Ben<br><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "JmWiuY75zFAJ",
   "from": "Sebastian Weber",
   "author": "97d82fedb7016adb",
   "date": "2015-07-28T11:35:08Z",
   "subject": "Re: rstan and packages",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Ben!<div><br></div><div>I followed what you wrote, but I am getting</div><div><br></div><div><div>Error in inDL(x, as.logical(local), as.logical(now), ...) : </div><div>  unable to load shared object </div><div><br></div><div>in my 32bit windows machine after a number of models have compiled. Is removing the compiled Stan object enough in order to unload the dll (which I assume each model represents)?</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks.</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><br></div><div>Sebastian</div><br>On Friday, July 24, 2015 at 3:35:54 PM UTC+2, Ben Goodrich wrote:</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">On Friday, July 24, 2015 at 2:48:29 AM UTC-4, Sebastian Weber wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I am planning to include stan models in R package and have a couple of questions:<div><br></div><div>- How can I make sure that the binary versions of the package come along with a pre-compiled stan binary?</div></div></blockquote><div><br>You have to compile them<br> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>- Is there a recommended way to do this?</div></div></blockquote><div><br>Download the .zip file but put it under inst/include of a copy of an unzipped rstan_2.7.0-1.tar.gz (not under the include/ directory of your installed rstan)<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">setwd</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;/path/to/inst/include/example-models&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>models </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"font-family:monospace\"><span style=\"color:#000000;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"color:#000\">dir</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">getwd</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> pattern </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;stan$&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> full</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">names</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> recursive </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>library</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">paralell</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></span><span style=\"color:#000\">options</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mc</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">cores </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">8</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#800\"># or however many cores you have available</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>output </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mclapply</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">models</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mc</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">silent </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>                   FUN </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">function</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">m</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">try</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rstan</span><span style=\"color:#660\">::</span><span style=\"color:#000\">stan_model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">m</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> auto_write </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>Then rebuild the rstan package via R CMD build<br><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>- Should I use the command-line interface or the rstan interface?</div></div></blockquote><div><br>rstan<br> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>- And which options make 100% sure that stan does not output anything (the open_progress=FALSE does not seem to be sufficient)</div></div></blockquote><div><br>The open_progress to sampling() or stan() is only relevant if you are running the model. Ostensibly the mc.silent argument suppresses stdout, but it is not as if the &quot;COMPILING MODEL X NOW&quot; statements hurt anything. You could also background the R process doing this.<br><br>Ben<br><br></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "I1gPdvhGE_kJ",
   "from": "Ben Goodrich",
   "author": "743bbacc59f4b25f",
   "date": "2015-07-28T11:42:01Z",
   "subject": "Re: rstan and packages",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Tuesday, July 28, 2015 at 7:35:08 AM UTC-4, Sebastian Weber wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Is removing the compiled Stan object enough in order to unload the dll (which I assume each model represents)?</div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div>No, you have to call dyn.unload(x) where x is given by the stanmodel&#39;s @dso@.CXXDSOMISC$dso_last_path.<br><br>Ben<br><br></div></div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "rGe4cS0kBwAJ",
   "from": "Sebastian Weber",
   "author": "97d82fedb7016adb",
   "date": "2015-07-29T08:29:52Z",
   "subject": "Re: rstan and packages",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Ben!<br><br>I just did what you suggested, but somehow stan_demo still wants to recompile things even though there is a rda file in place for each model. Any idea what went wrong and what I can do?<br><br>Thanks!<br><br>BTW, now the rstan package 160M large!<br><br>Best,<br>Sebastian</div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><br>On Tuesday, July 28, 2015 at 1:42:01 PM UTC+2, Ben Goodrich wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">On Tuesday, July 28, 2015 at 7:35:08 AM UTC-4, Sebastian Weber wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Is removing the compiled Stan object enough in order to unload the dll (which I assume each model represents)?</div></blockquote><div><br><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div>No, you have to call dyn.unload(x) where x is given by the stanmodel&#39;s @dso@.CXXDSOMISC$dso_last_path.<br><br>Ben<br><br></div></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "jguTMs71GgAJ",
   "from": "Ben Goodrich",
   "author": "743bbacc59f4b25f",
   "date": "2015-07-29T14:49:01Z",
   "subject": "Re: rstan and packages",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Wednesday, July 29, 2015 at 4:29:52 AM UTC-4, Sebastian Weber wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I just did what you suggested, but somehow stan_demo still wants to recompile things even though there is a rda file in place for each model. Any idea what went wrong and what I can do?<br></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>It even wants to recompile in a new R session? It is working for me. You could try the GitHub version since I made some changes after 2.7.0 was released. Otherwise, you would probably have to do debug(stan_model) and look at which line of<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">    </span><span style=\"color:#008\">if</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(!</span><span style=\"color:#000\">file</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">exists</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">file</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rda</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">||</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>       </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mtime</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rda </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> file</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">info</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">file</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rda</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\">$mtime</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  mtime </span><span style=\"color:#660\">||</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>       mtime</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rda </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">as</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#606\">POSIXct</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">packageDescription</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;rstan&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\">$Date</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">||</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>       mtime</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rda </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> rstan_load_time </span><span style=\"color:#660\">||</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>       </span><span style=\"color:#660\">!</span><span style=\"color:#008\">is</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">obj </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> readRDS</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">file</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rda</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;stanmodel&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">||</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>       </span><span style=\"color:#660\">!</span><span style=\"color:#000\">is_sm_valid</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">obj</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">||</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>       </span><span style=\"color:#660\">!</span><span style=\"color:#008\">is</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#008\">null</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">writeLines</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">obj@model_code</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> con </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> tf </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> tempfile</span><span style=\"color:#660\">()))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">||</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>       md5 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">!=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> tools</span><span style=\"color:#660\">::</span><span style=\"color:#000\">md5sum</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">tf</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>         </span><span style=\"color:#800\"># do nothing</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    </span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    </span><span style=\"color:#008\">else</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">return</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">invisible</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">obj</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>is unexpectedly failing.<br><br>Ben<br><div dir=\"ltr\"><div> </div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
